{
  "id": "MD-SB-281-2013",
  "scope": "state:md",
  "title": "SB 281 (Firearm Safety Act of 2013; Chapter 427) (2013 Regular) — Assault weapons ban (long guns and copycat), 10-round magazine limit, handgun qualification license, mental-health and dealer provisions, and other firearm changes",
  "urls": [
    "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/sb0281?ys=2013RS",
    "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/2013RS/Chapters_noln/CH_427_sb0281e.pdf",
    "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/2013RS/bills/sb/sb0281e.pdf",
    "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/2013RS/votes/Senate/1025.htm",
    "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/2013RS/votes/House/0895.htm"
  ],
  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "Crim. Law 4-303 assault weapon ban (on-topic rule): a person may not: (1) transport an assault [pistol] WEAPON into the State; or (2) possess, sell, offer to sell, transfer, purchase, or receive an assault [pistol] WEAPON.\nCrim. Law 4-305 magazine limit: A person may not manufacture, sell, offer for sale, purchase, receive, or transfer a detachable magazine that has a capacity of more than [20] 10 rounds of ammunition for a firearm.\nPub. Safety 5-117.1 handgun qualification license: A DEALER OR ANY OTHER PERSON MAY NOT SELL, RENT, OR TRANSFER A HANDGUN TO A PURCHASER, LESSEE, OR TRANSFEREE UNLESS THE PURCHASER, LESSEE, OR TRANSFEREE PRESENTS TO THE DEALER OR OTHER PERSON A VALID HANDGUN QUALIFICATION LICENSE\nPurpose clause: carry rules (handgun wear/carry/transport limits): altering the authorization for a person to wear, carry, or transport a handgun to be within certain limitations\nPurpose clause: mental-health prohibitions and relief; DHMH regulations: requiring certain persons to enter into a certain memorandum of understanding authorizing the Secretary of Health and Mental Hygiene to adopt certain regulations\nTitle text: hearing officer surrender order (domestic/protective orders): requiring a certain hearing officer, after making a certain determination, to order certain individuals to surrender or consign firearms in the individual's possession under certain circumstances\nTitle text: school-related changes: establishing a certain exception to the prohibition against carrying a deadly weapon on public school property\nTitle text: dealer licensing and records, lost or stolen reporting, registration of moved-in firearms: requiring the owner of a regulated firearm to report the loss or theft of the regulated firearm to a certain law enforcement agency within a certain period of time\nTitle text: Police Training Commission, sentencing offenses: making it a misdemeanor to use an assault long gun or a copycat weapon or a magazine that exceeds a certain maximum capacity of rounds of ammunition in the commission of a felony or a crime of violence\nSec 2 report by State Police: The Department of State Police shall investigate illegal transfers, possession, and transport of firearms within the State\nSec 3 effective date: this Act shall take effect October 1, 2013.\nCriminal Law 4-110 (new) -- 'restricted firearm ammunition' during a crime of violence (sentencing offence 1): A PERSON MAY NOT, DURING AND IN RELATION TO THE COMMISSION OF A CRIME OF VIOLENCE AS DEFINED IN § 14–101 OF THIS ARTICLE, POSSESS OR USE RESTRICTED FIREARM AMMUNITION.\nCriminal Law 4-306(b) -- using an assault weapon or a magazine over 10 rounds in a felony or crime of violence (sentencing offence 2): (b) (1) A person who uses an assault [pistol] WEAPON, or a magazine that has a capacity of more than [20] 10 rounds of ammunition, in the commission of a felony or a crime of violence ... is guilty of a misdemeanor\nHealth-General 10-632(g) (new) -- surrender order by hearing officer (this is the surrender order named in the title; it is not a domestic-violence order): IF A HEARING OFFICER ENTERS AN ORDER FOR INVOLUNTARY ADMISSION COMMITMENT UNDER PART III OF THIS SUBTITLE AND THE HEARING OFFICER DETERMINES THAT THE INDIVIDUAL CANNOT SAFELY POSSESS A FIREARM BASED ON CREDIBLE EVIDENCE OF DANGEROUSNESS TO OTHERS, THE HEARING OFFICER SHALL ORDER THE INDIVIDUAL\nPublic Safety 5-133(b) -- domestic-violence protective-order prohibition (the only protective-order text that is a prohibition): (I) A CURRENT NON EX PARTE CIVIL PROTECTIVE ORDER HAS BEEN ENTERED UNDER § 4–506 OF THE FAMILY LAW ARTICLE; OR (II) AN ORDER FOR PROTECTION, AS DEFINED IN § 4–508.1 OF THE FAMILY LAW ARTICLE, HAS BEEN ISSUED BY A COURT OF ANOTHER STATE OR A NATIVE AMERICAN TRIBE AND IS IN EFFECT",
